---
import ExamplePlan from '../../components/content/ExamplePlan.astro';
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';
import { examplesFor } from '../../lib/guides/examples';
import { cm, feetInches, len, range, size } from '../../lib/guides/measure';
import { withBase } from '../../lib/paths';

const slug = 'bedroom-layout';

// Standard US mattress sizes (inches).
const beds = [
  { name: 'Twin', w: 38, l: 75 },
  { name: 'Twin XL', w: 38, l: 80 },
  { name: 'Full (double)', w: 54, l: 75 },
  { name: 'Queen', w: 60, l: 80 },
  { name: 'King', w: 76, l: 80 },
  { name: 'California King', w: 72, l: 84 },
];

// The smallest floor area that keeps a 24 in walkway on both sides and at the foot of the bed.
const WALK = 24;
const minRooms = beds.map((b) => ({
  name: b.name,
  w: b.w + 2 * WALK,
  l: b.l + WALK,
}));
---

<Guide slug={slug}>
  <p class="text-lg text-slate-600">
    A bedroom layout comes down to three numbers: the size of the bed, the space you leave around
    it, and the room you need in front of storage. Below are the standard sizes, the clearances
    designers work with, and example floor plans you can open in the planner and adjust to your own
    room.
  </p>

  <h2 id="mattress-sizes">Standard mattress sizes</h2>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <caption>US mattress sizes, width × length</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Bed</th>
          <th scope="col">Mattress size</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {beds.map((b) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">{b.name}</th>
            <td>{size(b.w, b.l)}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="text-sm text-slate-600">
    These are mattress sizes. A frame, headboard or footboard usually adds a few inches, so measure
    your bed if you already have it. Sizes differ outside the US: a European double, for example, is
    usually 140 × 200 cm.
  </p>

  <h2 id="clearances">How much space to leave around a bed</h2>
  <p>These are the rules of thumb most designers use:</p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Where</th>
          <th scope="col">Minimum</th>
          <th scope="col">Comfortable</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Walkway along each side of the bed you get in from</th>
          <td>{len(24)}</td>
          <td>{range(30, 36)}</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Between the foot of the bed and a wall or furniture</th>
          <td>{len(24)}</td>
          <td>{len(36)}</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">In front of a dresser, to open the drawers and stand</th>
          <td>{len(36)}</td>
          <td>{len(42)}</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">In front of a closet or wardrobe</th>
          <td>{len(36)}</td>
          <td>{len(42)}</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    A bed pushed against a wall (common with twins and in small rooms) only needs a walkway on its
    open side. Keep the door's swing clear too: a hinged door sweeps a quarter circle as wide as the
    door, so a 32 in (81 cm) door needs 32 in of empty floor in front of it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="room-size">How big a room each bed needs</h2>
  <p>
    The smallest floor area that keeps a {len(WALK)} walkway on both sides and at the foot of the
    bed, with the headboard against a wall. Add the depth of any dresser plus {len(36)} in front of
    it.
  </p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Bed</th>
          <th scope="col">Clear floor (width × length)</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {minRooms.map((r) => (
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">{r.name}</th>
            <td>
              {feetInches(r.w / 12)} × {feetInches(r.l / 12)} ({cm(r.w)} × {cm(r.l)} cm)
            </td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    So a queen bed fits a 10 × 10 ft room. Put an 18 in deep dresser across from it and the room
    needs to be at least {feetInches((80 + 36 + 18) / 12)} long (80 + 36 + 18 in); the first example
    below is a 10 × 12 ft room laid out that way.
  </p>

  <h2 id="furniture-sizes">Nightstand and dresser sizes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Nightstands</strong> are usually {range(18, 24)} wide and {range(14, 18)} deep. Pick
      one whose top is about level with the top of your mattress.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Dressers</strong> (wide, low) run from about {range(30, 72)} wide and are{' '}
      {range(16, 20)} deep.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Chests of drawers</strong> (tall, narrow) are about {range(30, 40)} wide and the same
      depth as a dresser, so they save floor in a small room.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="tips">Layout tips</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Place the bed first.</strong> It is the largest piece. The usual spot is with the
      headboard against the longest solid wall, facing the door but not in the door's path.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Under a window is fine</strong> if the headboard is lower than the sill and you can
      still reach the window and any radiator or vent.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Check the paths:</strong> from the door to each side of the bed, to the closet and to
      the dresser. Each should be at least {len(24)} wide.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Measure the bed you have,</strong> including the frame, rather than using the mattress
      size.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="examples">Example bedroom layouts</h2>
  <p>
    Open any of these in the planner, then change the room size and the pieces to match your own.
    The planner warns you if a piece overlaps another, sticks out of the room or is in a door's way.
  </p>
  {examplesFor(slug).map((e) => (
    <ExamplePlan example={e} />
  ))}

  <p>
    Next: <a href={withBase('guides/studio-and-dorm-layout/')}>studio and dorm room layouts</a>, or
    the <a href={withBase('faq/')}>FAQ</a>.
  </p>
</Guide>
